ers between 0 (inclusive) and 5 (exclusive), call the ","key":"bSzb0YBJ5H"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"set_seed()","key":"GpG9zccNAk"},{"type":"text","value":" function and use ","key":"YaixcOt2GB"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"42","key":"hJytUxaR5J"},{"type":"text","value":" as the seed parameter.","key":"n4crI7DXuH"}],"key":"MHakuFd0JP"}],"key":"jLUBwNYGtH"},{"type":"break","key":"OtL93xLhAo"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4. Create a tensor “x” from the array above","key":"zOd4ki7JGV"}],"key":"FSkmJmSSJC"}],"key":"qrDJyZGYoU"},{"type":"break","key":"lRn1YGgU2V"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"5. Change the dtype of x from ","key":"KdIWXuxatc"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"int32","key":"KJn9vutNyv"},{"type":"text","value":" to ","key":"wfqduFLegY"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"int64","key":"pQ9qDzg66t"}],"key":"XJic4JCwDe"}],"key":"rapFBvFg3z"},{"type":"break","key":"FvPM3uDPDd"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"6. Reshape ","key":"Gi9YKPyVkm"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"D6TX5VyXnt"},{"type":"text","value":" into a 3x2 tensor","key":"qAg27wDd3n"}],"key":"N2D3ZP8LY6"},{"type":"text","value":" ","key":"Mu7OqJ8m9L"},{"type":"break","key":"UbzXNC9Z8M"},{"type":"text","value":"There are several ways to do this.","key":"Fn1mgPH9Lg"}],"key":"IHbGTdZypZ"},{"type":"break","key":"fsxC9baJiw"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"7. Return the right-hand column of tensor ","key":"Clmicm2mhE"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"Hx2f7ACudc"}],"key":"v6GPE3N5Fe"}],"key":"aJAhp5KBY6"},{"type":"break","key":"ZAAD4UmLrQ"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"8. Without changing x, return a tensor of square values of ","key":"rRDMbfaV6b"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"QavUZxxINr"}],"key":"NbFMo29eYC"},{"type":"text","value":" ","key":"mDtjBeE39E"},{"type":"break","key":"b5Wib8uXLt"},{"type":"text","value":"There are several ways to do this.","key":"xvPsxZ6d3G"}],"key":"yRrhjIjoZy"},{"type":"break","key":"Lapnf9Culy"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"9. Create a tensor ","key":"UmBSKmhylo"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y","key":"kACCLjRBwo"},{"type":"text","value":" with the same number of elements as ","key":"wh09R8apSo"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"KMNErYXrXc"},{"type":"text","value":", that can be matrix-multiplied with ","key":"gbw31CUBxw"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"a70iKSAvB3"}],"key":"lywqs8neYH"},{"type":"text","value":" ","key":"MnNcDbbsX4"},{"type":"break","key":"GpB2eEn5VB"},{"type":"text","value":"Use PyTorch directly (not NumPy) to create a tensor of random integers between 0 (inclusive) and 5 (exclusive). Use 42 as seed. ","key":"pWz5gM9JT2"},{"type":"break","key":"uwAetI74Ir"},{"type":"text","value":"Think about what shape it should have to permit matrix multiplication.","key":"ndfdHYSTrF"}],"key":"hSFBlo5mAY"},{"type":"break","key":"uUhRvYsG0j"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"10. Find the matrix product of ","key":"cDE7gdCx3e"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"ivGQ78ZbTG"},{"type":"text","value":" and ","key":"Hw8afFoaJP"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y","key":"vfXnQgLy1N"},{"type":"text","value":".","key":"WlQzMQXwhE"}],"key":"jOzMdqNGGq"}],"key":"GbDPEhaL87"}],"key":"wgdm24BiBe"}],"key":"YUS9Jfq3dh"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"1. Imports and Seed Initialization","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"jVdEbxXfPq"}],"identifier":"id-1-imports-and-seed-initialization","label":"1. Imports and Seed Initialization","html_id":"id-1-imports-and-seed-initialization","implicit":true,"key":"v4hAAR4b81"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This section sets up the foundational libraries required for tensor operations and mathematical computations. The set_seed() function is defined to ensure reproducible results across both NumPy and PyTorch environments by fixing the random number generators using torch.manual_seed(). This guarantees that “random” tensor generation will yield the exact same values every time the code runs.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"a7lglVH7fl"}],"key":"FHGBHv7kmi"}],"key":"RzlQT6uO97"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"# 1. Perform Standard Imports\nimport numpy as np\nimport torch\n\n# 2. Create a function called set_seed()\ndef set_seed(seed: int):\n    np.random.seed(seed)\n    torch.manual_seed(seed)","key":"rHe3sEiIIE"},{"type":"outputs","id":"jeQDmxGUeTtNiLxqdqE3d","children":[],"key":"lNbfm0tKLQ"}],"key":"eklAspOhzT"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"2. Array Generation, Tensor Conversion, and Reshaping","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"iDPyw4LpTU"}],"identifier":"id-2-array-generation-tensor-conversion-and-reshaping","label":"2. Array Generation, Tensor Conversion, and Reshaping","html_id":"id-2-array-generation-tensor-conversion-and-reshaping","implicit":true,"key":"bgNcPDimkB"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here, a NumPy array of 6 random integers is generated and immediately converted into a PyTorch tensor using torch.from_numpy(). Because PyTorch operations strictly require matching datatypes, the .type() method is explicitly used to cast the tensor from int32 to a 64-bit integer (torch.int64). Finally, the flat tensor is transformed into a 2D matrix (3 rows and 2 columns) using .reshape(), which safely alters the tensor’s view without modifying the original memory block.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"WrQNJlcSxi"}],"key":"K2i42XYUFc"}],"key":"ASM81b2smI"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"# 3. Create a NumPy array \nset_seed(42)\narr = np.random.randint(0, 5, 6)\n\n# 4. Create a tensor \"x\" from the array above\nx = torch.from_numpy(arr)\n\n# 5. Change the dtype of x from int32 to int64\nx = x.type(torch.int64)\n\n# 6. Reshape x into a 3x2 tensor\nx = x.reshape(3, 2)","key":"n5Yvk5ZJN8"},{"type":"outputs","id":"-DeOL6EQI41-KGa0ZWSfq","children":[],"key":"Tc1oPT4XGL"}],"key":"r6offkECAs"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"3. Tensor Slicing and Element-wise Operations","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"m53bfeK93y"}],"identifier":"id-3-tensor-slicing-and-element-wise-operations","label":"3. Tensor Slicing and Element-wise Operations","html_id":"id-3-tensor-slicing-and-element-wise-operations","implicit":true,"key":"VO63oEFBZ4"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This block demonstrates PyTorch’s native data manipulation capabilities. Extracting specific values from a tensor uses the exact same slicing syntax as NumPy arrays. The slice [:, 1:] grabs all rows (:) but only the elements from index 1 onwards (1:), effectively returning the right-hand column as a 2D slice. The .pow(2) function performs an element-wise arithmetic power operation, returning a brand new tensor containing the squared values without altering the original tensor x.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"VG2fd9FsM7"}],"key":"j4Tj5ssxle"}],"key":"ll5RRhPJkw"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"# 7. Return the right-hand column of tensor x\nright_column = x[:, 1:] \n\n# 8. Without changing x, return a tensor of square values of x\nx_squared = x.pow(2)","key":"nrJZltbemZ"},{"type":"outputs","id":"KNuuuwHPSEsM1MP2cOdXW","children":[],"key":"OeUdcEBbkQ"}],"key":"nTATbfDYPt"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"4. Matrix Creation and Multiplication","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"hGpOmlO50E"}],"identifier":"id-4-matrix-creation-and-multiplication","label":"4. Matrix Creation and Multiplication","html_id":"id-4-matrix-creation-and-multiplication","implicit":true,"key":"B4ZlrBLeOZ"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"To perform a 2D matrix multiplication, the number of columns in the first tensor must perfectly match the number of rows in the second tensor. Since tensor x has the shape (3, 2), the new tensor y is explicitly created from scratch using torch.randint() with an inverted shape of (2, 3). The operation ","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"JV3fcir45q"},{"type":"link","url":"http://torch.mm","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"torch.mm","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"Jvn8YccnPe"}],"urlSource":"http://torch.mm","key":"AHl1LAqbkK"},{"type":"text","value":"(x, y) then calculates the matrix product.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"lOsoK7CtNk"}],"key":"HWuWEuTFjV"}],"key":"lX070cyHEN"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"# 9. Create a tensor y with the same number of elements as x, that can be matrix-multiplied with x\nset_seed(42)\ny = torch.randint(0, 5, (2, 3))\n\n# 10. Find the matrix product of x and y\nmatrix_prod = torch.mm(x, y) \n\n# Print statements to verify the outputs\nprint(\"x:\\n\", x)\nprint(\"\\nright_column:\\n\", right_column)\nprint(\"\\nx_squared:\\n\", x_squared)\nprint(\"\\ny:\\n\", y)\nprint(\"\\nMatrix Product (x * y):\\n\", matrix_prod)","key":"jIlTWdfIHh"},{"type":"outputs","id":"gDwHDTr-qkoT528C8Iawo","children":[{"type":"output","jupyter_data":{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":"x:\n tensor([[3, 4],\n        [2, 4],\n        [4, 1]])\n\nright_column:\n tensor([[4],\n        [4],\n        [1]])\n\nx_squared:\n tensor([[ 9, 16],\n        [ 4, 16],\n        [16,  1]])\n\ny:\n tensor([[2, 2, 1],\n        [4, 1, 0]])\n\nMatrix Product (x * y):\n tensor([[22, 10,  3],\n        [20,  8,  2],\n        [12,  9,  4]])\n"},"children":[],"key":"vBw0ocmGwI"}],"key":"tRZn6dtsG8"}],"key":"W6l4mMrDDe"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tensor Reshaping and Slicing Verification","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"J7sigArFjf"}],"key":"aiBLhH9qRo"}],"key":"DUXcquO9mT"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The printed output of x confirms it was successfully reshaped into a 3x2 configuration. The right_column output [[4], [4], [1]] proves that the slicing operation [:, 1:] successfully extracted the final column while maintaining its proper 2D column-vector shape rather than flattening it into a 1D array.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"cORNWWiVtL"}],"key":"LzKthGTzIY"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Element-Wise Power Dynamics","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"iA4vvPubVY"}],"key":"S8mM4gjjFU"},{"type":"text","value":"\nThe x_squared output directly mirrors the shape of x, proving that basic arithmetic operations like pow() are applied on an element-by-element basis. Every individual scalar inside the matrix was multiplied by itself (e.g., the 3 became 9, the 4 became 16), entirely independently of the other elements in the tensor.   Dimensionality and the Matrix Product\nThe final output perfectly demonstrates the mathematical laws of matrix multiplication. Tensor x has a size of (3, 2) and tensor y has a size of (2, 3). Because their inner dimensions match (2 columns and 2 rows), the multiplication is valid. The resulting tensor natively adopts the outer dimensions of the two parent matrices, successfully outputting a new tensor with the expected shape of (3, 3).","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"cbi9EDqBiR"}],"key":"SNEhL431vE"}],"key":"w3XNJlelip"}],"key":"Q3N1uqhbHX"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}}}